lesson.body:
Every collection you have used so far, strings, lists, and tuples, remembers order: item zero always comes before item one. A set is a different kind of Data Type built around a different priority. A set does not track position at all, does not allow duplicate values, and is optimized for one question above all others: is this value in here?

fruits = {"apple", "banana", "apple", "cherry"}
print(len(fruits))
print(sorted(fruits))
print("banana" in fruits)

Even though the literal listed "apple" twice, len(fruits) prints 3, not 4, because a set automatically collapses duplicate values down to one. The next line prints ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']; sorted() is used here deliberately, because a set itself does not guarantee any particular order when you print or loop over it, so relying on the order you typed the values in would be a mistake. The final line prints True, confirming membership testing with in works on sets exactly the way it does on lists, but under the hood it is built on hashing rather than a position-by-position search, which is what makes checking membership in a large set so fast.

That hashing requirement is also what a set demands of anything you try to put inside it: every element has to be hashable, meaning Python can compute a fixed value from it that never changes. Immutable types like numbers, strings, and tuples qualify. Mutable types like lists do not, because their contents, and therefore their hash, could change after being added.

try:
    bad_set = {"a", [1, 2]}
except TypeError as e:
    print("Error:", e)

This prints Error: cannot use 'list' as a set element (unhashable type: 'list'), for exactly the reason you saw in Tuples vs Lists when a list could not be used as a dictionary key. Sets and dictionary keys share the same hashability requirement, which is why tuples, but not lists, can sit safely inside a set.
